#### Scope of the judge's guarantees (H3, H4)

The judge's text today (`docs/judge.md:68-69`) states G4 (restart order) and G5 (stop: "every child's final `post-stop` happens before the
parent's final `post-stop`; `post-stop` runs once per instance"). Their scope must not be widened:

| Relation | Applies to | Does not apply to |
|---|---|---|
| G4 | the order `pre-restart(old)`, `post-stop(old)`, `post-restart(new)`, `pre-start(new)` of a restart that completes | the cleanup of the old instance relative to its children (the default `preRestart` runs `post-stop(old)` before any child's `post-stop` has ended, **[Run]**) |
| G5 "children before the parent's final post-stop" | the **final** `post-stop` of an actor that terminates (termination rule) | `post-stop(old)` during a restart |
| G5 "`post-stop` runs once per instance" | nothing that holds in general: the runtime calls `post-stop` **once, at termination, on the current instance**; the *default* `preRestart` also calls it on the old instance, and a custom `preRestart` may call it zero or several times | a Stop that reaches an actor waiting for its children inside a restart whose default `preRestart` already called `post-stop`: **two calls observed** (S11); a `preRestart` that does not call it: **one** call, at termination, after the children |

**Accepted correction (owner, H4, 2026-10-14), to be applied in U7** (#449; the judge is not modified by this documentation work): reword
G5 to say "the final `post-stop`" for the children-first rule, and replace "runs once per instance" by "the runtime calls `post-stop`
once, at termination, on the current instance; the default `pre-restart` also calls it on the old instance", with a reference to S11,
so that G5 does not reject this valid Pekko behavior; add a Pekko-side scenario for S11 and its Go mirror (U7).
The Pekko documentation that G5 cites (`upstream/pekko/docs/src/main/paradox/actors.md:941-957`) describes the termination order
(children first, then `postStop`) and does **not** say "once per instance": that wording is the judge's own, and only the judge's
wording is contradicted by the observed double call.

